(function (root) {
  var DATA = {"schemaVersion":1,"topics":[{"id":"honeybees","subject":"Biology","title":"Honeybees and pollen","intro":"Honeybees live in large colonies and spend much of the warm season collecting food from flowers.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"A worker bee visits dozens of flowers on a single trip, and pollen sticks to the tiny hairs on her body.","ev":"Pollen sticks to the tiny hairs on a worker bee's body as she visits dozens of flowers on one trip.","tag":"pollen on a bee's body"},{"id":"f2","text":"When a bee lands on the next flower, some of that pollen rubs off onto the part of the flower that makes seeds.","ev":"Some pollen rubs off onto the seed-making part of the next flower a bee lands on.","tag":"pollen rubbing off on the next flower"},{"id":"f3","text":"Many fruits and vegetables, including apples and squash, only form when pollen reaches their flowers.","ev":"The source points out that foods such as apples and squash only form after pollen reaches their flowers.","tag":"apples and squash"},{"id":"f4","text":"A single hive can hold more than 20,000 bees, and thousands of them search for food each day.","ev":"A single hive can hold more than 20,000 bees, with thousands searching for food every day.","tag":"the size of a hive"},{"id":"f5","text":"Bees drink nectar, a sugary liquid inside flowers, and use it to make honey.","ev":"Bees drink nectar from flowers and use it to make honey.","tag":"honey production"},{"id":"f6","text":"Honeybees communicate by dancing, and the pattern of a dance tells other bees where flowers are located.","ev":"The pattern of a honeybee's dance tells hive mates where flowers are located.","tag":"the bees' dance"},{"id":"f7","text":"In winter, honeybees cluster together inside the hive and eat stored honey to stay warm.","ev":"In winter, honeybees cluster inside the hive and eat stored honey to stay warm.","tag":"winter clusters"},{"id":"f8","text":"A honeybee's wings beat about 200 times per second, which produces the buzzing sound people hear.","ev":"A honeybee's wings beat about 200 times per second, producing the buzzing sound people hear.","tag":"wing beats"}],"source":"01-topics-a.json"},{"id":"solar-panels","subject":"Energy and technology","title":"Solar panels","intro":"Solar panels are flat devices made of many small cells that capture energy from sunlight.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"Each cell in a solar panel is made of a material that produces an electric current when light strikes it.","ev":"A solar cell produces an electric current whenever light strikes its surface.","tag":"how cells react to light"},{"id":"f2","text":"Panels have no moving parts, so they make electricity quietly and need little repair.","ev":"Panels have no moving parts, so they work quietly and need little repair.","tag":"having no moving parts"},{"id":"f3","text":"Panels can be bolted onto rooftops, which lets a building use space that is otherwise empty.","ev":"Panels can be bolted onto rooftops, using space that is otherwise empty.","tag":"rooftop installation"},{"id":"f4","text":"On cloudy days, a panel still produces some electricity, though usually less than on a bright day.","ev":"The passage notes that even on cloudy days a panel produces some electricity, though less than in bright sunlight.","tag":"cloudy-day output"},{"id":"f5","text":"A solar panel does not burn fuel while it operates, so it gives off no smoke.","ev":"An operating panel burns no fuel and gives off no smoke.","tag":"not burning fuel"},{"id":"f6","text":"Some calculators contain tiny solar cells that let them run without batteries.","ev":"Certain calculators run on tiny solar cells instead of batteries.","tag":"solar calculators"},{"id":"f7","text":"Engineers measure a panel's output in watts, which describes how much power it can produce.","ev":"Engineers describe how much power a panel can produce by measuring its output in watts.","tag":"measuring output in watts"},{"id":"f8","text":"The first practical silicon solar cell was announced in 1954.","ev":"The first practical silicon solar cell was announced in 1954.","tag":"the 1954 solar cell"}],"source":"01-topics-a.json"},{"id":"water-cycle","subject":"Earth science","title":"The water cycle","intro":"Water on Earth is always moving between the oceans, the air, and the land in a pattern called the water cycle.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"The Sun warms the surface of oceans and lakes, and some liquid water changes into an invisible gas called water vapor.","ev":"Heat from the Sun turns some water from oceans and lakes into an invisible gas called water vapor.","tag":"evaporation from oceans and lakes"},{"id":"f2","text":"As water vapor rises, the air around it cools, and the vapor changes back into tiny droplets that form clouds.","ev":"Rising water vapor cools and changes back into tiny droplets that form clouds.","tag":"cloud formation"},{"id":"f3","text":"When droplets in a cloud join together and grow heavy, they fall to the ground as rain, snow, or hail.","ev":"Cloud droplets that join together and grow heavy fall as rain, snow, or hail.","tag":"precipitation"},{"id":"f4","text":"Rain that lands on hills and mountains flows downhill into streams and rivers, which carry the water toward the sea.","ev":"As the source describes, rain on hills and mountains flows downhill into streams and rivers that carry the water toward the sea.","tag":"runoff into rivers"},{"id":"f5","text":"Plants take in water through their roots and release some of it as vapor through tiny openings in their leaves.","ev":"Plants take in water through their roots and release part of it as vapor through their leaves.","tag":"plants releasing vapor"},{"id":"f6","text":"About 97 percent of Earth's water is salt water found in the oceans.","ev":"About 97 percent of Earth's water is salt water found in the oceans.","tag":"salt water in the oceans"},{"id":"f7","text":"Some rain soaks into the soil and becomes groundwater, which can stay underground for many years.","ev":"Some rain soaks into the soil and becomes groundwater that can stay underground for many years.","tag":"groundwater"},{"id":"f8","text":"Fog forms when water vapor cools into droplets close to the ground instead of high in the sky.","ev":"Fog forms when water vapor cools into droplets close to the ground.","tag":"fog"}],"source":"01-topics-a.json"},{"id":"arch-bridges","subject":"Engineering and architecture","title":"Arch bridges","intro":"An arch bridge uses a curved shape to carry the weight of the roadway and everything that travels across it.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"The curve of an arch pushes weight outward and downward along the curve, toward the supports at each end.","ev":"The curve of an arch pushes weight outward and downward toward the supports at each end.","tag":"how the curve carries weight"},{"id":"f2","text":"Stone arches built by Roman engineers about two thousand years ago still stand in parts of Europe.","ev":"Stone arches built by Roman engineers about two thousand years ago still stand in parts of Europe.","tag":"the Roman arches still standing"},{"id":"f3","text":"Because stone resists squeezing very well, a stone arch stays strong as long as its pieces are pressed tightly together.","ev":"A stone arch stays strong as long as its pieces are pressed tightly together, since stone resists squeezing very well.","tag":"stone resisting squeezing"},{"id":"f4","text":"The supports at the ends of an arch, called abutments, must be firmly anchored in the ground or in solid rock.","ev":"The supports at the ends of an arch, called abutments, must be firmly anchored in the ground or in solid rock.","tag":"abutments at the ends"},{"id":"f5","text":"Arch bridges can span wide gaps, such as rivers and valleys, without placing many supports in the water.","ev":"According to the text, arch bridges can span rivers and valleys without placing many supports in the water.","tag":"crossing wide gaps"},{"id":"f6","text":"Modern arches are often built from steel or concrete, which can be shaped into long, thin curves.","ev":"Modern arches are often built from steel or concrete, which can be shaped into long, thin curves.","tag":"steel and concrete arches"},{"id":"f7","text":"A bridge builder first constructs a temporary wooden frame, which holds the stones in place until the arch is complete.","ev":"Builders first make a temporary wooden frame that holds the stones in place until the arch is complete.","tag":"the temporary frame"},{"id":"f8","text":"Many arch bridges have a smooth, curved outline that people consider attractive.","ev":"Many arch bridges have a smooth, curved outline that people consider attractive.","tag":"the bridge's appearance"}],"source":"01-topics-a.json"},{"id":"public-libraries","subject":"Libraries and education","title":"Public libraries","intro":"A public library is a community resource that lends books and other materials to people who live nearby.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"With a library card, a person can borrow books for a few weeks at no charge and return them when finished.","ev":"With a library card, a person can borrow books for a few weeks at no charge.","tag":"borrowing books for free"},{"id":"f2","text":"Many libraries offer computers and internet access, which people can use for homework and job applications.","ev":"Many libraries provide computers and internet access that people can use for homework and job applications.","tag":"computers and internet access"},{"id":"f3","text":"Libraries host free programs, such as reading hours for young children and tutoring for students.","ev":"The passage says libraries host free programs, including reading hours for young children and tutoring for students.","tag":"free programs"},{"id":"f4","text":"Librarians help visitors find reliable sources and teach them how to search for information.","ev":"Librarians help visitors find reliable sources and teach them how to search for information.","tag":"help from librarians"},{"id":"f5","text":"Some libraries stay open late in the evening so that people who work during the day can visit.","ev":"Some libraries stay open late in the evening so that people who work during the day can visit.","tag":"evening hours"},{"id":"f6","text":"Library buildings often include quiet rooms for studying.","ev":"Library buildings often include quiet rooms for studying.","tag":"quiet study rooms"},{"id":"f7","text":"The oldest known libraries were collections of clay tablets in ancient Mesopotamia.","ev":"The oldest known libraries were collections of clay tablets in ancient Mesopotamia.","tag":"ancient libraries"},{"id":"f8","text":"Libraries buy books in large numbers and share each copy with many different readers.","ev":"Libraries buy books in large numbers and share each copy with many readers.","tag":"sharing copies among readers"}],"source":"01-topics-a.json"},{"id":"moon-phases","subject":"Astronomy","title":"Phases of the Moon","intro":"The Moon does not make its own light; it shines because it reflects sunlight toward Earth.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"The Moon orbits Earth, and half of it is always lit by the Sun, but the part of that lit half we can see changes as it moves.","ev":"The Moon orbits Earth, and the part of its lit half that we can see changes as it moves.","tag":"the Moon's orbit"},{"id":"f2","text":"A full cycle of phases, from one new moon to the next, takes about 29 days.","ev":"A full cycle of phases, from one new moon to the next, takes about 29 days.","tag":"the length of the cycle"},{"id":"f3","text":"During a new moon, the lit side faces away from Earth, so the Moon is nearly invisible in the night sky.","ev":"During a new moon, the lit side faces away from Earth, so the Moon is nearly invisible in the night sky.","tag":"the new moon"},{"id":"f4","text":"At full moon, we see the whole lit side of the Moon, and it appears as a bright circle.","ev":"At full moon, we see the whole lit side of the Moon as a bright circle.","tag":"the full moon"},{"id":"f5","text":"The Moon's surface is covered in craters formed by rocks that crashed into it long ago.","ev":"The Moon's surface is covered in craters formed by rocks that crashed into it long ago.","tag":"craters"},{"id":"f6","text":"Gravity on the Moon is about one sixth as strong as gravity on Earth.","ev":"Gravity on the Moon is about one sixth as strong as gravity on Earth.","tag":"the Moon's gravity"},{"id":"f7","text":"Ocean tides rise and fall mainly because of the pull of the Moon's gravity on Earth's water.","ev":"Ocean tides rise and fall mainly because of the pull of the Moon's gravity on Earth's water.","tag":"ocean tides"},{"id":"f8","text":"People have used the repeating phases of the Moon to build calendars for thousands of years.","ev":"The source mentions that people have used the repeating phases of the Moon to build calendars for thousands of years.","tag":"calendars"}],"source":"02-topics-b.json"},{"id":"cactus","subject":"Plants and animals","title":"Desert cacti","intro":"Cacti are plants that grow in deserts, where rain is rare and the sun is strong.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"A cactus stem is thick and spongy, and it stores large amounts of water after a rainfall.","ev":"The thick, spongy stem of a cactus stores large amounts of water after a rainfall.","tag":"the thick stem storing water"},{"id":"f2","text":"Instead of broad leaves, a cactus has narrow spines, which lose much less water to the dry air.","ev":"A cactus has narrow spines instead of broad leaves, so it loses much less water to the dry air.","tag":"spines instead of leaves"},{"id":"f3","text":"Many cacti have shallow roots that spread wide near the surface and quickly soak up rain before it dries up.","ev":"Many cacti have shallow roots that spread wide near the surface and quickly soak up rain.","tag":"shallow, wide-spreading roots"},{"id":"f4","text":"Spines also provide a little shade and keep some animals from eating the plant.","ev":"Spines also provide a little shade and keep some animals from eating the plant.","tag":"spines giving shade and protection"},{"id":"f5","text":"Some cacti can survive for a year or more without any rain.","ev":"Some cacti can survive for a year or more without any rain.","tag":"surviving without rain"},{"id":"f6","text":"The tallest cactus, the saguaro, can grow taller than a house with two stories.","ev":"The saguaro, the tallest cactus, can grow taller than a house with two stories.","tag":"the saguaro's height"},{"id":"f7","text":"Cactus flowers often open at night and are pollinated by bats and moths.","ev":"Cactus flowers often open at night and are pollinated by bats and moths.","tag":"night-blooming flowers"},{"id":"f8","text":"People grow cacti in pots because they need very little watering.","ev":"People grow cacti in pots because they need very little watering.","tag":"cacti as houseplants"}],"source":"02-topics-b.json"},{"id":"levers","subject":"Physics","title":"Levers","intro":"A lever is a simple machine made of a stiff bar that turns around a fixed point called a fulcrum.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"When the fulcrum is close to the load, a small push far from the fulcrum can lift a heavy object.","ev":"When the fulcrum is close to the load, a small push far from the fulcrum can lift a heavy object.","tag":"the fulcrum near the load"},{"id":"f2","text":"A crowbar lets a person pry up a nail by pushing down on the long end of the bar.","ev":"A person can pry up a nail with a crowbar by pushing down on the long end of the bar.","tag":"the crowbar"},{"id":"f3","text":"A seesaw is a lever with the fulcrum in the middle, so two children of equal weight can balance each other.","ev":"A seesaw is a lever with its fulcrum in the middle, so two children of equal weight can balance.","tag":"the seesaw"},{"id":"f4","text":"The farther you push from the fulcrum, the less force you need, but you must move your hand through a longer distance.","ev":"The text states that pushing farther from the fulcrum takes less force, but the hand must move through a longer distance.","tag":"force versus distance"},{"id":"f5","text":"Scissors are made of two levers joined at a shared fulcrum.","ev":"Scissors are made of two levers joined at a shared fulcrum.","tag":"scissors"},{"id":"f6","text":"People have used levers for thousands of years to move heavy stones.","ev":"People have used levers for thousands of years to move heavy stones.","tag":"the ancient use of levers"},{"id":"f7","text":"A wheelbarrow is a lever with the fulcrum at the wheel, so the person lifting the handles carries only part of the load.","ev":"A wheelbarrow is a lever with the fulcrum at the wheel, so the person lifting the handles carries only part of the load.","tag":"the wheelbarrow"},{"id":"f8","text":"A lever does not create energy; it only changes how effort is applied.","ev":"A lever does not create energy; it only changes how effort is applied.","tag":"levers and energy"}],"source":"02-topics-b.json"},{"id":"bicycle-gears","subject":"Transportation","title":"Bicycle gears","intro":"Most bicycles have gears, which are wheels with teeth that connect the pedals to the back wheel by a chain.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"In a low gear, each turn of the pedals moves the bike only a short distance, which makes pedaling easier on a steep hill.","ev":"In a low gear, each turn of the pedals moves the bike only a short distance, which makes a steep hill easier to climb.","tag":"low gears on hills"},{"id":"f2","text":"In a high gear, each turn of the pedals moves the bike a long distance, so a rider can reach a high speed on flat ground.","ev":"In a high gear, each turn of the pedals moves the bike a long distance, so a rider can reach a high speed on flat ground.","tag":"high gears on flat ground"},{"id":"f3","text":"A rider changes gears by moving a lever that shifts the chain from one toothed wheel to another.","ev":"A rider changes gears by moving a lever that shifts the chain from one toothed wheel to another.","tag":"shifting the chain"},{"id":"f4","text":"Many bikes have between seven and twenty-one gear combinations.","ev":"Many bikes have between seven and twenty-one gear combinations.","tag":"the number of gear combinations"},{"id":"f5","text":"A good gear choice lets a rider keep pedaling at a steady, comfortable pace.","ev":"A good gear choice lets a rider keep pedaling at a steady, comfortable pace.","tag":"a steady pedaling pace"},{"id":"f6","text":"Bicycle helmets are designed to absorb impact and protect the head.","ev":"Bicycle helmets are designed to absorb impact and protect the head.","tag":"helmets"},{"id":"f7","text":"The first bicycles, built in the early 1800s, had no pedals at all and were pushed along by the rider's feet.","ev":"The first bicycles, built in the early 1800s, had no pedals and were pushed along by the rider's feet.","tag":"early bicycles"},{"id":"f8","text":"Chains are usually oiled so they move smoothly across the teeth.","ev":"Chains are usually oiled so they move smoothly across the teeth.","tag":"oiling the chain"}],"source":"02-topics-b.json"},{"id":"rust","subject":"Chemistry","title":"Rust and iron","intro":"Rust is the reddish-brown coating that can appear on objects made of iron or steel.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen in the presence of water.","ev":"Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen in the presence of water.","tag":"iron reacting with oxygen and water"},{"id":"f2","text":"Rust is weaker than iron, so a badly rusted object can crumble or break.","ev":"Rust is weaker than iron, so a badly rusted object can crumble or break.","tag":"rust being weaker than iron"},{"id":"f3","text":"Painting a metal object keeps water and air away from the iron underneath.","ev":"Paint on a metal object keeps water and air away from the iron underneath.","tag":"painting the metal"},{"id":"f4","text":"Rust forms faster in damp places, and salty seawater makes the process even quicker.","ev":"Rust forms faster in damp places, and salty seawater makes the process even quicker.","tag":"damp and salty places"},{"id":"f5","text":"Stainless steel contains chromium, which forms a thin protective layer on the surface.","ev":"Stainless steel contains chromium, which forms a thin protective layer on its surface.","tag":"stainless steel"},{"id":"f6","text":"Steel is made mostly of iron, with small amounts of other materials added.","ev":"Steel is made mostly of iron, with small amounts of other materials added.","tag":"steel containing iron"},{"id":"f7","text":"Some metals, such as gold, do not rust at all.","ev":"Some metals, such as gold, do not rust at all.","tag":"gold not rusting"},{"id":"f8","text":"Gardeners often wipe their tools dry and coat them with oil after using them.","ev":"Gardeners often wipe their tools dry and coat them with oil after use.","tag":"oiled garden tools"}],"source":"02-topics-b.json"},{"id":"mars-rovers","subject":"Space exploration","title":"Mars rovers","intro":"Robotic rovers have driven across the surface of Mars to study its rocks, soil, and landscape.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"A radio message between Earth and Mars takes several minutes to travel, so engineers cannot steer a rover with a joystick.","ev":"Radio messages between Earth and Mars take several minutes to travel, so engineers cannot steer a rover with a joystick.","tag":"delays in radio messages"},{"id":"f2","text":"Rovers receive a list of instructions from Earth and then carry out the steps on their own, often during the Martian day.","ev":"Rovers receive a list of instructions from Earth and then carry out the steps on their own.","tag":"following instruction lists"},{"id":"f3","text":"Some rovers carry drills and cameras, which allow scientists to study rocks in detail from far away.","ev":"Some rovers carry drills and cameras that allow scientists to study rocks in detail from far away.","tag":"drills and cameras"},{"id":"f4","text":"The rover Curiosity landed on Mars in 2012 inside a huge basin called Gale Crater.","ev":"The rover Curiosity landed on Mars in 2012 inside a huge basin called Gale Crater.","tag":"Curiosity's landing"},{"id":"f5","text":"Rover wheels are made of metal and have grooves that help them grip loose sand and rocks.","ev":"Rover wheels are made of metal and have grooves that help them grip loose sand and rocks.","tag":"rover wheels"},{"id":"f6","text":"A day on Mars lasts about 24 hours and 37 minutes, slightly longer than a day on Earth.","ev":"A day on Mars lasts about 24 hours and 37 minutes, slightly longer than a day on Earth.","tag":"the length of a Martian day"},{"id":"f7","text":"In 2021, a small helicopter named Ingenuity made the first powered flight on another planet.","ev":"In 2021, a small helicopter named Ingenuity made the first powered flight on another planet.","tag":"the Ingenuity helicopter"},{"id":"f8","text":"Some rovers take pictures of the sky to measure how much dust is in the air.","ev":"Some rovers take pictures of the sky to measure how much dust is in the air.","tag":"photographing the sky"}],"source":"03-topics-c.json"},{"id":"printing-press","subject":"Inventions","title":"The printing press","intro":"Before the 1400s, books in Europe were copied by hand, a slow process that made each book rare and costly.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"Around 1450, Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing system in Germany that used movable metal letters.","ev":"Around 1450, Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing system in Germany that used movable metal letters.","tag":"movable metal letters"},{"id":"f2","text":"Each metal letter could be arranged into words, locked in a frame, and then reused for a different page.","ev":"Each metal letter could be arranged into words, locked in a frame, and then reused for a different page.","tag":"reusing the letters"},{"id":"f3","text":"A printer could produce hundreds of identical copies of a page using the same arranged letters.","ev":"A printer could produce hundreds of identical copies of a page from one arrangement of letters.","tag":"making identical copies"},{"id":"f4","text":"Within about fifty years, printing shops had opened in many cities across Europe.","ev":"Within about fifty years, printing shops had opened in many cities across Europe.","tag":"printing shops spreading"},{"id":"f5","text":"Printed books cost less than hand-copied ones, so more people could afford them.","ev":"The reading notes that printed books cost less than hand-copied ones, so more people could afford them.","tag":"lower book prices"},{"id":"f6","text":"Gutenberg's press was adapted from the screw presses already used to squeeze grapes and olives.","ev":"Gutenberg's press was adapted from screw presses already used to squeeze grapes and olives.","tag":"screw presses"},{"id":"f7","text":"Printing ink was thick and oily so that it would stick to the metal letters.","ev":"Printing ink was thick and oily so that it would stick to the metal letters.","tag":"oil-based ink"},{"id":"f8","text":"Early printed books often copied the style of handwritten pages.","ev":"Early printed books often copied the style of handwritten pages.","tag":"pages that imitated handwriting"}],"source":"03-topics-c.json"},{"id":"coral-reefs","subject":"Environmental science","title":"Coral reefs","intro":"A coral reef is a structure in warm, shallow ocean water built by tiny animals called corals.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"Reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet about a quarter of all ocean species depend on them at some point.","ev":"Reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet about a quarter of all ocean species depend on them at some point.","tag":"species that depend on reefs"},{"id":"f2","text":"Many young fish live among the branches of coral until they are large enough to survive in open water.","ev":"Many young fish live among the branches of coral until they are large enough to survive in open water.","tag":"young fish sheltering in coral"},{"id":"f3","text":"A reef forms a barrier that breaks up waves before they reach the shore.","ev":"A reef forms a barrier that breaks up waves before they reach the shore.","tag":"reefs breaking waves"},{"id":"f4","text":"Corals build hard skeletons of limestone, and over many years the skeletons pile up to form the reef.","ev":"Corals build hard skeletons of limestone, and over many years the skeletons pile up to form the reef.","tag":"limestone skeletons"},{"id":"f5","text":"Tiny algae live inside coral and make food from sunlight, which is why reefs grow in shallow, sunny water.","ev":"Tiny algae live inside coral and make food from sunlight, which is why reefs grow in shallow, sunny water.","tag":"algae living inside coral"},{"id":"f6","text":"Many reefs attract visitors who enjoy swimming and snorkeling.","ev":"Many reefs attract visitors who enjoy swimming and snorkeling.","tag":"snorkeling visitors"},{"id":"f7","text":"The largest reef system, the Great Barrier Reef near Australia, can be seen from space.","ev":"The largest reef system, the Great Barrier Reef near Australia, can be seen from space.","tag":"the Great Barrier Reef"},{"id":"f8","text":"Divers sometimes find reef animals in bright colors, including blue, orange, and yellow fish.","ev":"Divers sometimes find reef animals in bright colors, including blue, orange, and yellow fish.","tag":"colorful reef animals"}],"source":"03-topics-c.json"},{"id":"lightning","subject":"Weather and climate","title":"Lightning and thunder","intro":"Lightning is a giant spark of electricity that jumps through the air during some storms.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"Inside a storm cloud, ice crystals and water droplets collide, and these collisions separate electric charges within the cloud.","ev":"Inside a storm cloud, colliding ice crystals and water droplets separate electric charges.","tag":"colliding ice and droplets"},{"id":"f2","text":"When the difference in electric charge becomes large enough, a spark leaps between the cloud and the ground or between parts of the cloud.","ev":"When the difference in electric charge becomes large enough, a spark leaps between the cloud and the ground or within the cloud.","tag":"a spark leaping"},{"id":"f3","text":"A lightning bolt heats the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius, far hotter than the surface of the Sun.","ev":"A lightning bolt heats the surrounding air to about 30,000 degrees Celsius, far hotter than the surface of the Sun.","tag":"the extreme heat of a bolt"},{"id":"f4","text":"The heated air expands so quickly that it creates a booming sound wave, which people hear as thunder.","ev":"The heated air expands so quickly that it creates a booming sound wave that people hear as thunder.","tag":"thunder"},{"id":"f5","text":"Light travels much faster than sound, so people see a flash before they hear thunder.","ev":"The passage explains that light travels much faster than sound, so people see a flash before they hear thunder.","tag":"seeing lightning before hearing thunder"},{"id":"f6","text":"Counting the seconds between a flash and its thunder can tell a person roughly how far away the strike was.","ev":"Counting the seconds between a flash and its thunder can tell a person roughly how far away the strike was.","tag":"counting seconds"},{"id":"f7","text":"Tall, pointed objects such as trees and towers are more likely to be struck.","ev":"Tall, pointed objects such as trees and towers are more likely to be struck.","tag":"tall objects"},{"id":"f8","text":"Lightning rods are metal poles that give a strike a safe path to the ground.","ev":"Lightning rods are metal poles that give a strike a safe path to the ground.","tag":"lightning rods"}],"source":"03-topics-c.json"},{"id":"binary-numbers","subject":"Computer technology","title":"Binary numbers","intro":"Computers store and process all information, from photos to music, using only two symbols: 0 and 1.","facts":[{"id":"f1","text":"Each 0 or 1 is called a bit, and a bit can be represented by a switch that is either off or on.","ev":"Each 0 or 1 is called a bit, and a bit can be represented by a switch that is either off or on.","tag":"bits as off-or-on switches"},{"id":"f2","text":"Eight bits grouped together make one byte, which can form 256 different patterns.","ev":"Eight bits grouped together make one byte, which can form 256 different patterns.","tag":"bytes and patterns"},{"id":"f3","text":"Each letter and symbol on a keyboard is assigned its own number, which is stored as a pattern of bits.","ev":"The source states that each letter and symbol on a keyboard is assigned its own number, which is stored as a pattern of bits.","tag":"letters stored as numbers"},{"id":"f4","text":"In binary counting, each place is worth twice as much as the place to its right, such as 1, 2, 4, and 8.","ev":"In binary counting, each place is worth twice as much as the place to its right, such as 1, 2, 4, and 8.","tag":"binary place values"},{"id":"f5","text":"Electronic circuits can tell two states apart very reliably, such as high voltage and low voltage.","ev":"Electronic circuits can tell two states apart very reliably, such as high voltage and low voltage.","tag":"reliable two-state circuits"},{"id":"f6","text":"A kilobyte is about one thousand bytes, and a short email might take up a few kilobytes.","ev":"A kilobyte is about one thousand bytes, and a short email might take up a few kilobytes.","tag":"kilobytes"},{"id":"f7","text":"Early computers filled whole rooms and were operated using punched cards.","ev":"Early computers filled whole rooms and were operated using punched cards.","tag":"early computers"},{"id":"f8","text":"Colors on a screen are stored as numbers that tell how much red, green, and blue light to show.","ev":"Colors on a screen are stored as numbers that tell how much red, green, and blue light to show.","tag":"colors stored as numbers"}],"source":"03-topics-c.json"}],"models":[{"id":"honeybees-m1","family":"process","level":"beginner","q":"How do honeybees help flowering plants reproduce?","restate":"Honeybees help flowering plants reproduce in an important way.","answer":"Honeybees carry pollen from flower to flower, which allows plants to make seeds.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["Because the hairs hold the pollen, the bee is carrying plant material from one flower toward the next.","Pollen that reaches the seed-making part of a flower can start the process of forming seeds."],"rep":["A bee's body hairs hold pollen as she travels among dozens of flowers.","Pollen rubs off onto flowers when a bee lands on them."],"sum":"In short, honeybees move pollen between flowers, and that movement lets plants make seeds.","sumNew":"In short, honeybees move pollen between flowers, and they also huddle in the hive during winter to eat stored honey.","bad":"Honeybees help plants reproduce by planting seeds in the soil beneath each flower.","badRestate":"Honeybees spend the warm season gathering food for their colonies.","off":"f5","offExp":"Because nectar is sugary, bees can turn it into honey that feeds the colony.","topicId":"honeybees"},{"id":"honeybees-m2","family":"cause-effect","level":"intermediate","q":"Why are honeybees important for the food people eat?","restate":"Honeybees play a part in providing the food that people eat.","answer":"Honeybees matter for food supplies because many crops depend on the pollen they spread.","ev":["f3","f4"],"exp":["Since these foods depend on pollen, the bees that deliver it help make the harvest possible.","With so many bees working each day, pollen can be spread across a very large number of flowers."],"rep":["Apples and squash form only when pollen reaches their flowers.","A hive holds more than 20,000 bees, and thousands search for food daily."],"sum":"The pollen-spreading work of honeybees supports the production of foods that people rely on.","sumNew":"The pollen-spreading work of honeybees supports our food, and their wings beat about 200 times per second.","bad":"Honeybees matter for food because they grow apples and squash inside their hives.","badRestate":"Honeybees make a buzzing sound that people hear whenever the bees fly.","off":"f8","offExp":"Because the wings beat so quickly, the air vibrates and people hear a steady buzz.","topicId":"honeybees"},{"id":"solar-panels-m1","family":"function","level":"beginner","q":"What makes solar panels a practical way to make electricity?","restate":"Solar panels are a practical way to make electricity because of how they work and where they can go.","answer":"Solar panels are practical because they turn light directly into electricity and fit onto spaces that already exist.","ev":["f1","f3"],"exp":["That means sunlight itself becomes the source of power, with no extra step such as burning something.","Using a roof lets a building produce power without needing a new piece of land."],"rep":["Light that strikes a cell causes it to produce an electric current.","Panels are placed on rooftops that would otherwise be empty."],"sum":"Altogether, panels are practical because they change light into electricity and can sit on spaces buildings already have.","sumNew":"Altogether, panels are practical because they change light into electricity, and engineers rate each one in watts.","bad":"Solar panels are practical because they store enough energy to power a building for a year without sunlight.","badRestate":"Panels come in many sizes and are rated by how much power they can produce.","off":"f7","offExp":"Because output is measured in watts, buyers can compare how much power different panels produce.","topicId":"solar-panels"},{"id":"solar-panels-m2","family":"evaluation","level":"intermediate","q":"Is a solar panel a dependable source of electricity?","restate":"Whether a solar panel is dependable depends on how it is built and how it performs in different weather.","answer":"Solar panels are fairly dependable because they have few parts that can wear out and still work in weak light.","ev":["f2","f4"],"exp":["With nothing mechanical to break down, a panel is less likely to stop working than a machine with many moving pieces.","Producing at least some power under clouds means electricity does not disappear whenever the sky is not clear."],"rep":["Panels contain no moving parts and work quietly with little repair.","A cloudy day still produces some electricity, but less than a bright day."],"sum":"The simple design and partial cloudy-day output together make solar panels a reasonably dependable source of electricity.","sumNew":"The simple design and partial cloudy-day output make panels dependable, and they also burn no fuel or give off smoke.","bad":"Solar panels are dependable because they make exactly the same amount of electricity every hour of the day.","badRestate":"Solar panels are helpful because they do not burn fuel or give off smoke while operating.","off":"f5","offExp":"Since no fuel is burned, the panel itself does not release smoke into the air around it.","topicId":"solar-panels"},{"id":"water-cycle-m1","family":"process","level":"beginner","q":"How does ocean water end up as clouds and rain?","restate":"Water from the ocean can become clouds and rain through a series of steps.","answer":"Heat lifts ocean water into the air as vapor, and cooling turns that vapor into the clouds that produce rain.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["Without this first step, liquid water would stay in the ocean and never enter the air.","Clouds are where the scattered vapor gathers again as droplets, linking the water in the air to the rain that falls."],"rep":["The Sun's heat changes some of the water from oceans and lakes into water vapor.","Vapor that rises into cooler air changes into tiny droplets that make up clouds."],"sum":"In short, the Sun's heat lifts water into the air, and cooling turns it into the clouds that bring rain.","sumNew":"In short, the Sun's heat lifts water into the air and cooling forms clouds, and about 97 percent of Earth's water is salty ocean water.","bad":"Ocean water becomes rain when the wind pushes the waves up into the sky until they freeze.","badRestate":"Most of the water on Earth is found in the oceans.","off":"f6","offExp":"Because so much of Earth's water is in the oceans, most of the planet's water is salty.","topicId":"water-cycle"},{"id":"water-cycle-m2","family":"pattern","level":"intermediate","q":"Why does water on Earth keep moving instead of staying in one place?","restate":"Earth's water keeps moving from place to place rather than staying in one spot.","answer":"Water keeps moving because the Sun's heat lifts it into the air and falling rain carries it back toward the sea.","ev":["f1","f4"],"exp":["Since the Sun keeps warming oceans and lakes, water is repeatedly lifted out of them as vapor.","Because the water flows downhill, rain that falls on high ground is carried back toward the sea, which completes the loop."],"rep":["The Sun warms oceans and lakes, and some of the water becomes invisible vapor.","Rain that lands on mountains flows downhill into streams and rivers."],"sum":"Water is always on the move because the Sun lifts it into the air and flowing rain returns it.","sumNew":"Water is always on the move because the Sun lifts it into the air and flowing rain returns it, and some even soaks into the ground for many years.","bad":"Water keeps moving because the ocean is pulled from one side of the planet to the other by the Moon.","badRestate":"Some rain sinks into the ground instead of flowing to streams and rivers.","off":"f7","offExp":"Because groundwater can stay underground for years, some of the water leaves the visible cycle for a long time.","topicId":"water-cycle"},{"id":"arch-bridges-m1","family":"adaptation","level":"beginner","q":"Why is an arch a strong shape for a bridge?","restate":"The arch shape gives a bridge a great deal of strength.","answer":"An arch is strong because its curve directs the weight of the bridge toward solid supports.","ev":["f1","f3"],"exp":["Sending the load along the curve means that no single spot has to hold up all of the weight alone.","Pressing the stones together uses the very kind of force that stone handles best."],"rep":["The curve of an arch pushes weight outward and downward toward the end supports.","A stone arch remains strong as long as its pieces are pressed together tightly."],"sum":"An arch stays strong because its curve guides weight to the supports and keeps the stone pieces pressed together.","sumNew":"An arch stays strong because its curve guides weight to the supports, and its smooth outline is considered attractive.","bad":"An arch is strong because the roadway on top of it is made from the hardest stone in the world.","badRestate":"The look of an arch bridge is one reason people enjoy these structures.","off":"f8","offExp":"Since people find the smooth outline pleasing, an arch can make a bridge look graceful.","topicId":"arch-bridges"},{"id":"arch-bridges-m2","family":"evaluation","level":"intermediate","q":"What advantages do arch bridges offer for crossing rivers and valleys?","restate":"Arch bridges offer certain advantages when builders need to cross rivers and valleys.","answer":"Arch bridges suit wide crossings because they can stretch across gaps and can last for centuries.","ev":["f5","f2"],"exp":["Needing fewer supports in the water means builders face fewer difficulties working in the river itself.","Structures that still stand after about two thousand years show how durable a well-built arch can be."],"rep":["Arch bridges can cross rivers and valleys without many supports in the water.","Roman stone arches from about two thousand years ago still stand in parts of Europe."],"sum":"Overall, arches suit rivers and valleys because they cross wide gaps and can remain in use for a very long time.","sumNew":"Overall, arches suit rivers and valleys because they cross wide gaps and last, and builders use a temporary wooden frame while constructing them.","bad":"Arch bridges are the best choice for crossing any gap because they never need any repairs.","badRestate":"Builders construct a temporary frame to hold the stones while an arch is built.","off":"f7","offExp":"Because the frame supports the stones during construction, builders can place each piece safely before the arch is finished.","topicId":"arch-bridges"},{"id":"public-libraries-m1","family":"function","level":"beginner","q":"How do public libraries help students learn?","restate":"Public libraries support students' learning in several ways.","answer":"Libraries help students learn by giving them free access to reading materials and to tools for schoolwork.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["Since the books cost nothing to borrow, students can read widely without spending any money.","A student who has no computer at home can still finish assignments that require one."],"rep":["A person with a library card can borrow books at no charge for a few weeks.","Libraries provide computers and internet access for homework and job applications."],"sum":"Libraries help students learn by offering free books and the technology needed for assignments.","sumNew":"Libraries help students learn by offering free books and technology, and the oldest known libraries held clay tablets.","bad":"Libraries help students learn by writing each student's homework assignments for them.","badRestate":"Libraries have a long history that began with collections of clay tablets.","off":"f7","offExp":"Because clay tablets were stored together, ancient communities could keep their records in one place.","topicId":"public-libraries"},{"id":"public-libraries-m2","family":"evaluation","level":"intermediate","q":"Why is a library a valuable resource for a whole community?","restate":"A library serves an entire community, not only people who already read often.","answer":"A library is valuable to a whole community because it offers programs and expert help that people of many ages can use.","ev":["f3","f4"],"exp":["Reading hours for young children and tutoring for students show that the library serves different age groups.","Guidance in finding reliable sources helps visitors judge information, a skill useful at any age."],"rep":["Libraries host free programs, including reading hours and tutoring.","Librarians help visitors locate reliable sources and show them how to search."],"sum":"By offering programs and guidance, a library benefits people of every age in the whole community.","sumNew":"By offering programs and guidance, a library benefits the whole community, and its buildings often have quiet rooms for studying.","bad":"A library is valuable to a community because it pays visitors to read books every day.","badRestate":"Many library buildings include quiet rooms where visitors can study.","off":"f6","offExp":"Because the rooms are quiet, students can concentrate while they study.","topicId":"public-libraries"},{"id":"moon-phases-m1","family":"cause-effect","level":"beginner","q":"Why does the Moon look different from night to night?","restate":"The Moon's appearance changes from one night to the next.","answer":"The Moon looks different because we see a different amount of its sunlit side as it travels around Earth.","ev":["f1","f3"],"exp":["Since the Moon keeps moving along its path, the angle from which we view its bright half is always changing.","When the bright half points away from us, almost nothing of the Moon can be seen, which is the darkest point of its cycle."],"rep":["The Moon orbits Earth, and the visible part of its lit half changes as it moves.","A new moon happens when the lit side faces away from Earth, so the Moon is almost invisible."],"sum":"The Moon's changing look comes from how much of its lit half faces Earth as it orbits.","sumNew":"The Moon's changing look comes from how much of its lit half faces Earth, and its surface is covered with craters formed long ago.","bad":"The Moon looks different because Earth's shadow slowly covers a little more of it each night.","badRestate":"The Moon's surface looks different up close because of its many craters.","off":"f5","offExp":"The marks left by past collisions explain why the Moon's surface looks rough up close.","topicId":"moon-phases"},{"id":"moon-phases-m2","family":"pattern","level":"advanced","q":"How can the repeating pattern of the Moon's phases be useful to people?","restate":"The repeating pattern of the Moon's phases can serve people in practical ways.","answer":"The regular pattern of phases is useful because it lets people predict the Moon's appearance and keep track of time.","ev":["f2","f8"],"exp":["Because each cycle lasts about the same length, anyone who counts nights can estimate when the next new moon will arrive.","Since the same cycle returns again and again, people could count on it as a dependable way to organize their months."],"rep":["One cycle from a new moon to the next lasts about 29 days.","Calendars have been based on the Moon's repeating phases for thousands of years."],"sum":"Overall, the Moon's predictable cycle gives people a natural way to anticipate its appearance and measure the passing of time.","sumNew":"Overall, the Moon's predictable cycle helps people measure time, and the Moon's gravity is about one sixth as strong as Earth's.","bad":"The Moon's phases are useful because they control when each season begins on Earth.","badRestate":"The Moon's gravity is much weaker than Earth's, which affects how objects behave there.","off":"f6","offExp":"Because the Moon's gravity is weaker, an object there would weigh less than it does on Earth.","topicId":"moon-phases"},{"id":"cactus-m1","family":"adaptation","level":"beginner","q":"How is a cactus able to survive in a desert?","restate":"A cactus is able to live in a place that gets very little rain.","answer":"A cactus survives in the desert because its body is built to store water and to lose very little of it.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["Having a reserve of stored water allows the plant to keep living through the long periods between rainstorms.","With less water escaping through spines than broad leaves would allow, the plant keeps more of what it has stored."],"rep":["After a rainfall, the thick, spongy stem of a cactus holds a large amount of water.","A cactus has narrow spines instead of broad leaves, so it loses less water to dry air."],"sum":"A cactus survives desert conditions because it keeps a supply of water and wastes very little.","sumNew":"A cactus survives desert conditions by keeping water, and its flowers often open at night for bats and moths.","bad":"A cactus survives in the desert because its roots reach water hundreds of feet underground.","badRestate":"Cactus flowers often open at night, which helps the plant get pollinated.","off":"f7","offExp":"Flowers that open at night can attract nighttime visitors such as bats and moths.","topicId":"cactus"},{"id":"cactus-m2","family":"process","level":"advanced","q":"Why can a cactus make good use of a brief rainstorm?","restate":"A cactus is able to benefit from rain that lasts only a short time.","answer":"A cactus uses a brief storm well because it absorbs water quickly and holds on to what it takes in.","ev":["f3","f1"],"exp":["Roots that stay near the surface and spread wide catch the water as soon as it soaks into the soil, before the sun dries it out.","Because the stem keeps the absorbed water inside, the plant can use it slowly after the storm is over."],"rep":["Shallow roots spread wide near the surface and soak up rain for many cacti.","A cactus stem is thick and spongy and stores large amounts of water after rainfall."],"sum":"A cactus makes the most of short storms by soaking up water fast and storing it for later.","sumNew":"A cactus makes the most of short storms by soaking up water fast, and its spines also keep some animals from eating it.","bad":"A cactus uses brief storms well because its spines can make rain fall on the plant.","badRestate":"Spines help protect a cactus by keeping some animals from eating it.","off":"f4","offExp":"Since spines cast a little shade, the plant's surface stays slightly cooler in the strong sun.","topicId":"cactus"},{"id":"levers-m1","family":"function","level":"beginner","q":"How does a lever make heavy objects easier to lift?","restate":"A lever changes how much effort a person needs to lift a heavy object.","answer":"A lever makes lifting easier by letting a small force applied far from the fulcrum move a heavy load.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["Placing the fulcrum near the load gives the person leverage, so a little effort becomes a large lifting force.","The nail resists being pulled out, yet the long end of the bar lets a modest push overcome that resistance."],"rep":["A small push far from a fulcrum that is close to the load can lift a heavy object.","A crowbar lets someone push down on its long end to pry up a nail."],"sum":"In short, a lever helps by turning a small push far from the fulcrum into enough force to move a heavy load.","sumNew":"In short, a lever turns a small push into enough force to move a heavy load, and a seesaw is a lever with its fulcrum in the middle.","bad":"A lever makes lifting easier by creating extra energy that the person did not supply.","badRestate":"A seesaw balances two children by placing a fulcrum between them.","off":"f3","offExp":"Because the fulcrum is in the middle, a seesaw balances when the weights on both sides match.","topicId":"levers"},{"id":"levers-m2","family":"tradeoff","level":"advanced","q":"What does a person give up when a lever makes a task need less force?","restate":"It is worth understanding what a person gives up when a lever lowers the force a task needs.","answer":"A person gives up distance, because the easier the push becomes, the farther the hand must travel.","ev":["f4","f8"],"exp":["Since the hand must travel through a longer distance, the smaller force is paid for with extra motion.","Because no energy is created, the lever cannot reduce the force a person needs without asking for something in return."],"rep":["Pushing farther from the fulcrum takes less force but a longer movement of the hand.","A lever only changes how effort is applied and does not create energy."],"sum":"A lever trades force for distance rather than producing any extra energy.","sumNew":"A lever trades force for distance, and scissors use two levers that share a fulcrum.","bad":"A person gives up strength, because using a lever always makes the user weaker over time.","badRestate":"Scissors use two levers that share a fulcrum, which gives them cutting power.","off":"f5","offExp":"Because the two blades pivot on one shared point, they cut by squeezing the material between them.","topicId":"levers"},{"id":"bicycle-gears-m1","family":"function","level":"beginner","q":"Why do bicycles have more than one gear?","restate":"Many bicycles come with several gears instead of just one.","answer":"Bicycles have several gears so riders can adjust how hard they pedal for different kinds of ground.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["On a steep hill, an easier gear keeps the rider from having to push very hard just to keep climbing.","On level ground, a harder gear turns each pedal stroke into more distance, letting the rider build up speed."],"rep":["A low gear moves the bike a short distance with each turn of the pedals.","A high gear moves the bike a long distance with each turn of the pedals."],"sum":"In short, several gears let a rider match the effort of pedaling to hills and flat roads alike.","sumNew":"In short, several gears let a rider match effort to the road, and the first bicycles from the early 1800s had no pedals.","bad":"Bicycles have several gears because the chain would break if it only used one.","badRestate":"The first bicycles looked very different from the ones people ride today.","off":"f7","offExp":"Since early bicycles had no pedals, riders had to push themselves along with their feet.","topicId":"bicycle-gears"},{"id":"bicycle-gears-m2","family":"tradeoff","level":"advanced","q":"How does choosing the right gear help a rider stay comfortable?","restate":"The gear a rider chooses can affect how comfortable a ride feels.","answer":"The right gear keeps the effort of pedaling steady, whatever the slope of the road.","ev":["f5","f1"],"exp":["Pedaling at a steady pace avoids sudden bursts of effort that would tire a rider quickly.","Making each turn easier on a hill keeps the rider's pace from slowing to a struggle."],"rep":["A good gear lets a rider keep a steady, comfortable pedaling pace.","A low gear moves the bike a short distance per pedal turn on a steep hill."],"sum":"Choosing a gear to suit the slope lets a rider pedal at a steady and comfortable pace.","sumNew":"Choosing a gear to suit the slope keeps pedaling comfortable, and chains are usually oiled to move smoothly.","bad":"The right gear keeps a rider comfortable because it makes the bicycle weigh less on hills.","badRestate":"Oiling a bicycle chain helps it move smoothly across the teeth.","off":"f8","offExp":"Oil reduces friction, so the chain slides over the teeth with less resistance.","topicId":"bicycle-gears"},{"id":"rust-m1","family":"cause-effect","level":"beginner","q":"What causes rust to form on an iron object?","restate":"Rust forming on an iron object has a specific cause that can be identified.","answer":"Rust forms when iron is exposed to both oxygen and water.","ev":["f1","f4"],"exp":["Since iron, oxygen, and water must all meet, an iron object kept dry is much less likely to rust.","Damp air and salty water supply the moisture that speeds up the change, so objects near the sea rust quickly."],"rep":["Iron reacts with oxygen in the presence of water to form rust.","Rust forms faster in damp places, and salty seawater speeds it up even more."],"sum":"Rust develops when iron meets oxygen and water, and extra moisture makes it happen faster.","sumNew":"Rust develops when iron meets oxygen and water, but gold avoids the problem because it does not rust.","bad":"Rust forms when iron is heated until it glows red-hot in the open air.","badRestate":"Some metals never rust, no matter how long they are left outside.","off":"f7","offExp":"Because gold does not rust, it can keep its shine without any protection.","topicId":"rust"},{"id":"rust-m2","family":"function","level":"advanced","q":"How can people protect iron objects from rust?","restate":"People can take steps to keep iron objects from rusting.","answer":"People protect iron by keeping water and oxygen from reaching its surface.","ev":["f3","f5"],"exp":["Because rust needs both water and air, a coating that blocks them stops the change before it starts.","The thin layer formed by chromium acts like a built-in coating, so stainless steel protects itself."],"rep":["Painting a metal object keeps water and air away from the iron below.","Stainless steel contains chromium, which forms a thin layer on the surface."],"sum":"Blocking the contact between iron and the water and air it needs is how rust can be prevented.","sumNew":"Blocking the contact between iron and the water and air it needs prevents rust, and rusted iron is weaker than ordinary iron.","bad":"People protect iron by leaving it in damp, salty places so that rust cannot spread.","badRestate":"Rust damages iron objects by making them weaker over time.","off":"f2","offExp":"Since rust is weaker than iron, a badly rusted beam may no longer hold as much weight.","topicId":"rust"},{"id":"mars-rovers-m1","family":"cause-effect","level":"intermediate","q":"Why do Mars rovers need to carry out instructions on their own?","restate":"Mars rovers need to carry out instructions on their own instead of being steered directly.","answer":"Rovers act on their own because messages from Earth arrive too slowly for real-time control.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["A joystick only works if the rover responds right away, which cannot happen when every signal needs minutes to cross space.","Receiving a full list in advance means the rover can keep working even while it waits for the next message."],"rep":["Radio messages need several minutes to travel between Earth and Mars.","Rovers get a list of instructions from Earth and then carry out the steps on their own."],"sum":"The slow trip of radio messages is why rovers must follow instructions independently.","sumNew":"The slow trip of radio messages is why rovers work independently, and their metal wheels have grooves for gripping loose sand.","bad":"Rovers act on their own because their batteries cannot power a radio receiver.","badRestate":"Rovers are built with special wheels to travel across the rough Martian ground.","off":"f5","offExp":"Grooved metal wheels allow a rover to keep moving across loose ground without slipping much.","topicId":"mars-rovers"},{"id":"mars-rovers-m2","family":"process","level":"advanced","q":"How do rovers help scientists learn about Mars?","restate":"Rovers contribute to what scientists have learned about Mars.","answer":"Rovers help scientists by carrying instruments to places people cannot yet visit and gathering detailed observations.","ev":["f3","f8"],"exp":["Examining rocks through a rover's drills and cameras lets scientists study the planet closely while staying on Earth.","Measuring dust in the air gives scientists information about the Martian atmosphere that no one on Earth could collect directly."],"rep":["Some rovers have drills and cameras for studying rocks from far away.","Certain rovers photograph the sky to find out how much dust is in the air."],"sum":"Rovers act as the scientists' eyes and tools on Mars, collecting data about both the ground and the sky.","sumNew":"Rovers act as the scientists' tools on Mars, and in 2021 a helicopter named Ingenuity flew there for the first time.","bad":"Rovers help scientists by bringing Martian rocks back to Earth so they can be tested in laboratories.","badRestate":"A small helicopter made the first powered flight on another planet in 2021.","off":"f7","offExp":"A powered flight above the surface showed that flying machines could operate on another planet.","topicId":"mars-rovers"},{"id":"printing-press-m1","family":"process","level":"intermediate","q":"How did movable metal letters make printing faster?","restate":"Movable metal letters changed how quickly books could be made.","answer":"Movable letters sped up printing because they could be reused and could produce many copies at once.","ev":["f2","f3"],"exp":["Since the same letters could be rearranged for the next page, printers did not have to make a new set each time.","Making copies from one arrangement meant a page did not have to be written out again for every reader."],"rep":["Metal letters could be arranged into words, locked in a frame, and reused on another page.","One arrangement of letters could produce hundreds of identical page copies."],"sum":"Reusable letters and repeatable copies let printers make books much faster than hand copying.","sumNew":"Reusable letters let printers work faster, and a thick, oily ink was used so it would stick to the metal.","bad":"Movable letters sped up printing because they wrote the words on their own without any human help.","badRestate":"The ink used in printing needed to stick well to metal letters.","off":"f7","offExp":"A sticky ink was needed so that the letters would leave a clear mark on the paper.","topicId":"printing-press"},{"id":"printing-press-m2","family":"cause-effect","level":"advanced","q":"Why did the printing press lead to more people owning books?","restate":"The printing press made it possible for many more people to own books.","answer":"The press led to wider book ownership because it lowered prices and let printing spread quickly to new places.","ev":["f5","f4"],"exp":["When a book costs less, families with modest incomes can buy one instead of leaving books to the wealthy few.","As printing shops opened in more cities, books could be made close to the readers who wanted them."],"rep":["Printed books cost less than hand-copied books, so more people could buy them.","Printing shops had opened in many European cities within about fifty years."],"sum":"Overall, cheaper books and a growing number of printing shops put books within reach of many more people.","sumNew":"Overall, cheaper books and more printing shops put books within reach of many people, and Gutenberg adapted his press from screw presses used for grapes.","bad":"The press led to wider ownership because every printed book was given away for free.","badRestate":"Gutenberg's press borrowed ideas from machines that were already used on farms.","off":"f6","offExp":"Reusing an existing machine design helped Gutenberg build his press without inventing every part from scratch.","topicId":"printing-press"},{"id":"coral-reefs-m1","family":"scale","level":"intermediate","q":"Why are coral reefs important to ocean life?","restate":"Coral reefs matter a great deal to the life of the ocean.","answer":"Coral reefs are important because they provide homes and shelter for a large share of ocean animals.","ev":["f1","f2"],"exp":["A tiny fraction of the sea floor supporting so many species means that damage to a reef would affect a great deal of ocean life.","Shelter among the coral protects young fish while they grow, so more of them can reach adulthood."],"rep":["Reefs cover under one percent of the ocean floor, but about a quarter of ocean species rely on them.","Young fish often live in the branches of coral until they can survive in open water."],"sum":"Reefs deserve attention because so many ocean animals depend on them for shelter and growth.","sumNew":"Reefs deserve attention because so many animals depend on them, and many reefs attract visitors who enjoy snorkeling.","bad":"Coral reefs are important because they produce most of the oxygen in the atmosphere.","badRestate":"Many people travel to reefs to enjoy swimming and snorkeling.","off":"f6","offExp":"Visitors who snorkel above a reef can see the wildlife without having to dive deep.","topicId":"coral-reefs"},{"id":"coral-reefs-m2","family":"process","level":"advanced","q":"How does a coral reef come to protect a nearby shore?","restate":"The way a coral reef comes to protect a nearby shore can be explained.","answer":"A reef protects the shore because corals build a solid barrier that weakens approaching waves.","ev":["f4","f3"],"exp":["Because the skeletons pile up over many years, the reef grows into a large, solid structure rather than a thin layer.","Waves that lose energy against the reef arrive at the beach with less force than they began with."],"rep":["Limestone skeletons built by corals pile up over many years to form a reef.","A reef acts as a barrier that breaks up waves before they reach land."],"sum":"By growing into a barrier that takes the force out of waves, a reef shields the shore.","sumNew":"By growing into a barrier, a reef shields the shore, and tiny algae inside the corals make food from sunlight.","bad":"A reef protects the shore because its bright colors scare large waves away.","badRestate":"Corals grow in sunny water because algae living inside them need light.","off":"f5","offExp":"Since the algae make food from sunlight, coral can grow well only where light reaches it.","topicId":"coral-reefs"},{"id":"lightning-m1","family":"cause-effect","level":"intermediate","q":"What causes the sound of thunder?","restate":"The sound of thunder has a cause that can be explained.","answer":"Thunder is caused by air that lightning heats so fast that it expands violently.","ev":["f3","f4"],"exp":["Such an enormous jump in temperature gives the surrounding air no time to adjust gradually.","The sudden expansion pushes air outward in a wave, and that moving wave is what reaches our ears as a boom."],"rep":["A lightning bolt heats the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius.","Heated air expands quickly and makes a sound wave that people hear as thunder."],"sum":"In short, thunder is the sound of air that was heated and pushed outward by a lightning bolt.","sumNew":"In short, thunder is the sound of air pushed outward by a bolt, and metal lightning rods guide strikes safely to the ground.","bad":"Thunder is caused by storm clouds crashing into each other like heavy objects.","badRestate":"Lightning rods are used on buildings to guide strikes toward the ground.","off":"f8","offExp":"A metal pole offers a path that carries the electricity safely away from a building.","topicId":"lightning"},{"id":"lightning-m2","family":"scale","level":"advanced","q":"How can a person estimate how far away a lightning strike was?","restate":"There is a way for a person to estimate how far away a lightning strike was.","answer":"A person can estimate the distance by timing the gap between the flash and the thunder.","ev":["f5","f6"],"exp":["Because light arrives almost instantly, the flash marks the moment of the strike, and the thunder follows later.","A longer count means the sound had farther to travel, so the strike must have been farther away."],"rep":["Light travels much faster than sound, so a flash is seen before thunder is heard.","Counting the seconds between a flash and thunder gives a rough idea of the distance."],"sum":"The delay between flash and thunder gives people a simple way to judge how far away a strike was.","sumNew":"The delay between flash and thunder shows how far away a strike was, and tall, pointed objects are more likely to be hit.","bad":"A person can estimate the distance by measuring how bright the flash looks at night.","badRestate":"Tall, pointed objects such as trees and towers are struck more often than shorter objects.","off":"f7","offExp":"Since tall, pointed objects stand above their surroundings, a bolt is more likely to reach them first.","topicId":"lightning"},{"id":"binary-numbers-m1","family":"function","level":"intermediate","q":"Why do computers use only two symbols to store information?","restate":"Computers rely on just two symbols to represent every kind of information.","answer":"Computers use two symbols because circuits can reliably tell two states apart and group them into larger patterns.","ev":["f5","f2"],"exp":["If a circuit only has to choose between two states, mistakes are less likely than if it had to tell many levels apart.","Grouping eight bits gives hundreds of patterns, so two symbols are enough to represent a great variety of things."],"rep":["Electronic circuits can reliably tell two states apart, such as high and low voltage.","A byte is eight bits and can form 256 different patterns."],"sum":"Two reliable symbols, grouped into patterns, give computers all they need to store information.","sumNew":"Two reliable symbols grouped into patterns are enough for computers, and early computers were run with punched cards.","bad":"Computers use two symbols because larger sets of symbols cannot be shown on a screen.","badRestate":"Early computers were very large machines that were operated with punched cards.","off":"f7","offExp":"Because they relied on punched cards, early computers had to be given information in a very different way.","topicId":"binary-numbers"},{"id":"binary-numbers-m2","family":"process","level":"advanced","q":"How can a computer store written text using only numbers?","restate":"The way a computer stores written text using only numbers can be explained.","answer":"A computer stores text by giving each character a number and recording that number as a pattern of bits.","ev":["f3","f4"],"exp":["Because every character has its own number, the computer can keep a letter by recording its number instead of its shape.","Place values let a row of 0s and 1s stand for larger numbers, so even a long list of characters can be written in two symbols."],"rep":["Each keyboard letter and symbol is given a number stored as a pattern of bits.","In binary counting, each place is worth twice the place to its right, such as 1, 2, 4, and 8."],"sum":"Overall, text can be stored because characters become numbers and numbers can be written as patterns of bits.","sumNew":"Overall, text becomes numbers and then bits, and screen colors are also stored as numbers for red, green, and blue light.","bad":"A computer stores text by taking a tiny photograph of each letter and saving the picture.","badRestate":"A computer stores screen colors by saving how much red, green, and blue light to show.","off":"f8","offExp":"Since colors are stored as numbers, the screen can recreate a color by showing the right amount of each light.","topicId":"binary-numbers"}],"families":["adaptation","cause-effect","evaluation","function","pattern","process","scale","tradeoff"],"subjects":["Astronomy","Biology","Chemistry","Computer technology","Earth science","Energy and technology","Engineering and architecture","Environmental science","Inventions","Libraries and education","Physics","Plants and animals","Space exploration","Transportation","Weather and climate"]};
  if (typeof module === 'object' && module.exports) module.exports = DATA;
  else { root.RACECES = root.RACECES || {}; root.RACECES.CONTENT = DATA; }
})(typeof self !== 'undefined' ? self : this);
